@(33 = 33-39) @q{“The one who (?) rises (?) …… at dawn (?) from among the …… who dwell in ……, the @fsux{lukur} priestess of @wDN{Ninurta} from the ……, has thrown ……. Like a mourning (?) god (?), she ……. Her shouts (?) …… evil. In the midst of the cloister (?) she (?) …….  @gap{1 line fragmentary}  …… has made (?) the widespread people …… grain and …….”}

@(40 = 40-46) @q{“The confusion of battles …… the @fsux{nin-diŋir} priestess of @wDN{Nuska}. She tears apart (?) …… for you, …… for you her (?) …….  @gap{5 lines fragmentary} ”}

@(47 = 47-55) @q{“Your sons who were treated (?) like king's sons, what do they (?) eat ……, what do they (?) drink ……? They (?) …… honey and ghee. They load the table with (?) oil for you. The tears which they shed for him are tears of compassion. Their mourning (?) for him is that of loving and pure-hearted ones. They are bent over like over-ripe grain. The fledglings return ……, raise …….”}

@(56 = 56-62) @q{“Over the brides of your sons who have said @q{“Where, where is he now?”} your …… has fallen. In their …… has been silenced (?) for you. On the laps of the household …… for you. Your …… sweet sounds …… sleep …… has been …… like ……. The …… lament for you …… does not (?) cease.”}

@(63 = 63-69) @q{“O my father, may your heart be at rest. O @wPN{@wDN{Nanna}ya}, may your spirit be pleased. The @fsux{en} and @fsux{ensi} ……. Those who have escaped the hand of death ……. The hand of death has been …… in their ……. Death is the favour of the gods, the place where fate is decreed ……. May your offspring …… your knee.”}

@(70 = 70-76) @q{“Your daughters have …… for you in their ……. The elders of your city have set up (?) mourning (?) for you. The young women of your city have …… for you. The slave at the grindstone …… has shed (?) tears for you. The house where he is placed (?) ……. He has …… silver, has acquired grain, has …… wide possessions. May the eldest son …… for you your …… firm foundations.”}

@(77 = 77-84) @q{“As for your murderer who …… like one who …… the heart, your …… who plotted malice against you -- true (?) execution belongs to the king, the shepherd, your god; true (?) counsel belongs to @wDN{Utu}. May that man be a man accursed, death ……. …… his bones. May the names of his offspring ……. May their possessions …… like birds flying away.”}

@(85 = 85-87) @q{“May the …… of the Land ……. May he bring you favourable …… words, may they make you content. O @wPN{@wDN{Nanna}ya}, may your spirit be pleased, may your heart be at rest.”}

@(88 = 88-98) @q{“@wDN{Utu}, the great lord of the nether world, after turning the dark places to light, will judge your case. May @wDN{Nanna} decree your fate on the day of sleep. @wDN{Nergal}, the @wDN{Enlil} of the underworld, …… before it, may the …… utter your name, may he cause you to eat fresh food. May you be …… of the underworld, and may she have pity on you. May your household bring fresh water to the libation place. May Lord @wDN{Ninŋišzida} …… the house ……. May the mighty @wDN{Gilgameš} …… health for you. May @wDN{Neti} and @wRN{Etana} be your helpers. May the god of the underworld utter prayers for you.”}

@(99 = 99-103) @q{“May your god say @q{“Enough!”}, may he …… your fate. May the god of your city …… compassion on you. May he release you from wrath and sin. May he erase the reckoning of the guilt of your house. …… the evil planned against you …….”}

@(104 = 104-112) @q{“May your descendants be happy, ……. May ……. May the protective gods and goddesses …… your ……. May the children you begot be written down for privilege (?). May your daughters have divine favour. May your wife stay well, may your kin multiply. May prosperity and well-being (?) surround them day in day out. May good beer never cease in your libation tube. May the  @varh[t552.v3]{invocation }@vart[t552.v4,t552.v3]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} incantation } of your house be forever the  @varh[t552.v5]{invocation }@vart[t552.v6,t552.v5]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} incantation } of your god.”}

